==English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:yesterday}}
From {{inh|en|enm|yesterday}}, {{m|enm|yisterday}}, {{m|enm|ȝesterdai}}, {{m|enm|ȝisterdai}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġiestrandæġ}}, {{m|ang|ġister dæġ}}, {{m|ang|ġestor dæġ}}, {{m|ang|ġeostran dæġ|t=yesterday}}, {{surf|+pre|en|yester|day|nocap=1}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|yisterday}}, {{m|sco|yesterday|t=yesterday}}, {{cog|stq|jässendeeg}}, {{m|stq|järsendeges|t=yesterday|pos=adverb}}, {{cog|fy|justerdei|t=yesterday}}, {{cog|nl|gisterdag|t=yesterday}}, dialectal {{cog|de|gestertag|t=yesterday}}, {{cog|sv|gårdag|t=yesterday}}, {{cog|got|𐌲𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍂𐌰𐌳𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃|t=tomorrow|pos=adverb}}. Compare further {{cog|nl|gisteren|t=yesterday}}, {{cog|de|gestern|t=yesterday}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈjɛstədeɪ/|/ˈjɛstədɪ/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|En-uk-yesterday.ogg|a=RP}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈjɪstədə/|a=Northumbria}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈjɛstɹdeɪ/|/ˈjɛstɹdi/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-yesterday.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈjɪstɹdeɪ/|/ˈjɪstɹdi/|a=dated,Southern US folk speech|ref2={{R:en:PEAS|5.2|urlpage=134|pages=134-135}}}}
* {{hyphenation|en|yes|ter|day}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[day]] immediately before [[today]]; one day ago.
#: {{ux|en|Today is the child of '''yesterday''' and the parent of tomorrow.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Yesterday''' was rainy, but by this morning it had begun to snow.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1899|author=w:Hughes Mearns
|title={{w|Antigonish (poem)|Antigonish}}
|passage='''Yesterday''', upon the stair&nbsp;/ I met a man who wasn’t there&nbsp;/ He wasn’t there again today&nbsp;/ I wish, I wish he’d go away&nbsp;&hellip;}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=21 November 1929|title=Miss Mars to Wed H. Grell Powers: Her Troth to New Yorker Is Announced by Her Father at a Luncheon at Park Lane.{{nb...|Miss Murphy Betrothed: Granddaughter of Late U.&nbsp;S. Senator Is to Marry Montague H. Duval, Publisher.}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250910042822/https://www.nytimes.com/1929/11/21/archives/miss-mars-to-wed-h-grell-powers-her-troth-to-new-yorker-is.html|page=23|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_new-york-times_1929-11-21_79_26234_0/page/n22/mode/1up|volume=LXXIX|issue=26,234|column=6|passage=At a luncheon given '''yesterday''' at the Park Lane by Andrew H. Mars of Brooklyn for the Misses Marjorie Heather and Miriam Snyder, announcement was made of the engagement of his daughter, Miss Gladys L. Mars, to H. Grell Powers of this city.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1965|author=[[w:Paul McCartney|Paul McCartney]]|title=[[w:Yesterday (song)|Yesterday]] |album=[[w:Help!|Help!]]|text='''Yesterday''' / All my troubles seemed so far away / Now it looks as though they're here to stay / Oh, I believe in '''yesterday'''}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=w:Sheryl Gay Stolberg|title=Bush Mourns 9/11 at Ground Zero as N.Y. Remembers|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110926114926/https://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/11/nyregion/nyregionspecial3/11bush.html|date=11 September 2006|passage=Across the city '''yesterday''', there was a feeling of bittersweet reunion as streams of humanity converged and mingled at dozens of memorial services.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The [[past]], often in terms of being [[outdated]].
#: {{ux|en|'''yesterday's''' technology}}
#: {{ux|en|The worker of today is different from that of '''yesterday'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|5|5|text=All our '''yesterdays''' have lighted fools the way to dusty death.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=76|magazine=w:The Economist
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21579801-making-sense-statistics-riddle-our-days-snakes-and-ladders|title=Snakes and ladders
|passage=Risk is everywhere. From tabloid headlines insisting that coffee causes cancer ('''yesterday''', of course, it cured it) to stern government warnings about alcohol and driving, the world is teeming with goblins. For each one there is a frighteningly precise measurement of just how likely it is to jump from the shadows and get you.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130810095914/http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21579801-making-sense-statistics-riddle-our-days-snakes-and-ladders}}

====Usage notes====
* The plural {{m|en|yesterdays}} is unusual and often poetic for the recent past, e.g. “all our yesterdays have come back to haunt us”.
* While pronunciations with {{IPAchar|/ˈjɪ-/}} are now dialectal, they were formerly found in the standard language. For example, writer and [[orthoepist]] {{w|Thomas Sheridan (actor)|Thomas Sheridan}} prescribed such a pronunciation in his work.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Thomas Sheridan|title=A Complete Dictionary of the English Language, Both with Regard to Sound and Meaning|volume=2|url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=pJoRAAAAIAAJ|year=1790|publisher=C. Dilly}}</ref>

