====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|usually|_|in the plural}} [[sanity|Sanity]].
#: {{ux|en|He's gone completely out of his '''wits'''.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|_|in the plural}} The [[sense]]s.
#: {{ux|en|Keep your '''wits''' about you.}}
# Intellectual ability; faculty of thinking, reasoning.
#: {{ux|en|Where she has gone to is beyond the '''wit''' of man to say.}}
# The ability to think quickly; mental cleverness, especially under short time constraints.
#: {{ux|en|My father had a quick '''wit''' and a steady hand.}}
# Intelligence; common sense.
#: {{ux|en|The opportunity was right in front of you, and you didn't even have the '''wit''' to take it!}}
#* {{RQ:Wakefield Mystery Plays|play=Creation|page=6|lines=174–179|passage=I gif the '''witt''', I gif the strenght, / of all thou sees, of brede & lengthe; / thou shall be wonder wise. / Myrth and Ioy to haue at will, / All thi likyng to fulfill, / and dwell in paradise.|t=I give thee consciousness, I give thee strength over all thou seest, over all its breadth and length thou shalt be wondrously wise. Mirth and joy [you shall] have '''at will''' to fulfil all thy liking and dwell in paradise.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=31|passage=Wel, wel (''Meander'') thou art deepely read:<br>And hauing thee, I haue a iewell ſure:<br>Go on my Lord, and giue your charge I ſay,<br>Thy '''wit''' wil make vs Conquerors to day.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|23|text=O, learn to read what silent love hath writ:<br>To hear with eyes belongs to love's fine '''wit'''.}}
# [[humour|Humour]], especially when clever or quick.
#: {{ux|en|The best man's speech was hilarious, full of '''wit''' and charm.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=152|volume=III|text='''Wit''' is just as much put upon—blamed for a thousand impertinences over which it would not have held for a moment its glittering shield; it is like the radiant fairy doomed to wander over earth, concealed and transformed, and only allowed on rare occasions to shine forth in its true and sparkling form. It is well that '''wit''' is an impalpable and ethereal substance, or it must long since have evaporated in indignation at that peculiarly wretched and mistaken race, its imitators.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=8|passage=The humor of my proposition appealed more strongly to Miss Trevor than I had looked for, and from that time forward she became her old self again;{{...}}. Our table in the dining-room became again the abode of scintillating '''wit''' and caustic repartee, Farrar bracing up to his old standard, and the demand for seats in the vicinity rose to an animated competition.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|episode=w:Scenes from the Class Struggle in Springfield|title=w:The Simpsons|season=7|number=14|writer=w:Jennifer Crittenden|date=04 February 1996|note=production code 3F13|passage=''Evelyn Peters:'' "Don't worry, Marge. Her idea of '''wit''' is nothing more than an incisive observation humorously phrased and delivered with impeccable timing".}}
#* {{RQ:Fry Liar|page=26|passage={{...}} the [[cemetery]] – which people of shattering '''wit''' like Sampson never tired of calling ‘the [[dead centre]] of town’ {{...}}}}
# A person who tells funny anecdotes or jokes; someone witty.
#: {{ux|en|Your friend is quite a '''wit''', isn't he?}}
#* {{RQ:Jonson Poetaster|act=III|scene=iv|url=https://archive.org/stream/poetasterorarrai00jons_0#page/n62/mode/1up|passage=''Tuc''[''ca'']. {{...}} Can thy Author doe it impudently enough? / ''Hiſt''[''rio'']. O, I warrant you, Captaine: and ſpitefully inough too; he ha's one of the moſt ouerflowing villanous '''wits''', in ''Rome''. He will ſlander any man that breathes; If he diſguſt him. / ''Tucca''. I'le know the poor, egregious, nitty Raſcall; and he haue ſuch commendable Qualities, I'le cheriſh him: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=IV|page=301|passage={{...}} here were no Gibers, Cenſurers, Backbiters, Pick-pockets, Highwaymen, Houſebreakers, Attorneys, Bawds, Buffoons, Gameſters, Politicians, '''Wits''', ſplenetick tedious Talkers, Controvertiſts, Raviſhers, Murderers, Robbers, Virtuoſo's; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1989|month=May|volume=175|number=5|title=The Baltic: Arena of Power|author=Priit J. Vesilind|page=632
|text=A group of young '''wits''' from the resort town of Pärnu sells cans of seawater as gag gifts; on the labels they list the high pollution levels and give medical instructions.}}

