=====Usage notes=====
* Since the 2010s, ''wit'' has come to be increasingly used in continental Dutch among youth and others (associated with social justice movements) as a more neutral alternative to the most commonly used {{l|nl|blank}}, which is argued to be tainted by the colonial era (see {{cog|af|blank}}) and have a connotation of "cleanliness" and "purity" that ''wit'' does not have. See ''{{w|nl:Blank en wit in het racismedebat|Blank en wit in het racismedebat}}'' on nlwiki.

