https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0988589729| author=Robert McGowan| year=2013| passage=It is a crop, like a crop of corn, which differs from a natural field of grasses in the way that a crop of trees differs from a '''woods'''.}}
# {{lb|en|military|attributive}} For chemical behavior purposes, trees in full leaf (coniferous or medium-dense deciduous forests).

====Usage notes====
* The word ''woods'' in the sense of a woodland more often takes a plural verb or determiner (as in ''these woods are'') than a singular verb or determiner (as in ''this woods is'').<ref>[https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=%28this+woods+is*50%29%2Cthese+woods+are&year_start=1800&year_end=2000&corpus=15&smoothing=9&share=&direct_url=t1%3B%2C%28this%20woods%20is%20%2A%2050%29%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Cthese%20woods%20are%3B%2Cc0 Ngram Viewer] finds "this woods is" to have been about 1/50th as common as "these woods are" since the 1960s, and historically rarer. Compare [https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=%28a+woods+is*150%29%2Cthe+woods+are&year_start=1800&year_end=2000&corpus=15&smoothing=9&share=&direct_url=t1%3B%2C%28a%20woods%20is%20%2A%20150%29%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Cthe%20woods%20are%3B%2Cc0 "a woods is"], 1/150th as common as "the woods are".</ref>
* In some {{w|varieties of English}}, only the [[plurale tantum]] form of the word is used in the sense of a woodland; thus, one does not say ''"I was lost in the '''wood'''"'' but rather ''"I was lost in the '''woods''',"'' and one does not speak of going ''from this wood to that wood'' but rather ''from these woods to those woods''.

