#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:George Jones (navy chaplain)|George Jones]]|chapter=No. 271. December 30th, 1854: Morning.|title=Observations on the Zodiacal Light, from April 2, 1853, to April 22, 1855, Made Chiefly on Board the United States Steam-Frigate Mississippi, during Her Late Cruise in Eastern Seas, and Her Voyage Homeward: With Conclusions from the Data thus Obtained|series=[[w:Narrative of the Expedition of an American Squadron to the China Seas and Japan|Narrative of the Expedition of an American Squadron to the China Seas and Japan,{{nb...|Performed in the Years 1852, 1853, and 1854, under the Command of M. C. Perry, United States Navy, by Order of the Government of the United States}}]]|seriesvolume=III; 33d [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], 2d Session, [[w:United States Senate|Senate]] Ex. Doc. no. 79|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=Beverley Tucker, senate printer|year=1856|page=542|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FOoKCnXDZogC&pg=PA542|oclc=837795162|passage=I also, this morning gave attention to the stars as seen through the '''Zodiacal Light''', and found, even to 4{{sup|h}} 30{{sup|m}}, when the effulgent Light below the zigzag lines is very strong, that with the naked eye I could readily make out stars of the 6th magnitude within the effulgent Light; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Wells Island of Doctor Moreau|chapter=The Thing in the Forest|pages=81–82|pageref=81|passage=Westward I saw the '''zodiacal light''' mingling with the yellow brilliance of the evening star.}}

